  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "carnatic-music-tunes-forms-history-and-recordings",
      "title": "Carnatic Music — Tunes — Forms, History, and Recordings",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** South Asian Traditions | **Region:** south-carnatic | **Tier:** 4.5\n**Prerequisites:** regions-south-carnatic-07-representative-depth\n**Tags:** south, carnatic, south-asia, tunes, —"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Kriti repertoire\n\nCarnatic Music belongs to the **South Asian Traditions** curriculum. M. S. Subbulakshmi\n\nThis lesson develops listening precision for **Kriti repertoire** — connecting repertoire, instruments, dance, and the institutions that transmit music today.\n\n![Traditional musicians](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Fiddle.jpg?width=640)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "compared-with-hindustani-tradition",
      "title": "Compared with Hindustani tradition",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Hindustani Music** (`regions-south-hindustani-08-tunes`) covers the same lesson slot from the anchor perspective. This lesson stresses: **Kriti repertoire**.\n\n| Topic | Hindustani tradition anchor | Carnatic Music (this lesson) |\n|-------|------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Focus | Yaman, Bhairavi listening… | Kriti repertoire… |\n\n**Parallel anchor lesson:** `regions-south-hindustani-08-tunes`\n**Secondary comparison (Ireland):** `regions-celtic-ireland-08-tunes`"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "core-material",
      "title": "Core material",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Kriti repertoire\n\nStudy **carnatic music** through listening first. Wikipedia's article on [Carnatic music](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carnatic_music) orients geography and history; recordings prove how musicians phrase ornaments, tempo, and ensemble balance in real rooms."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tune-studies",
      "title": "Tune studies",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Representative repertoire includes **Kriti repertoire**. Use the lesson tune panel and **Play all** to compare multiple settings of the same title or form."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "regional-accent-and-social-context",
      "title": "Regional accent and social context",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Tradition lives in **community practice** — festivals, dances, teaching networks, and archives — not only on concert stages. For **South Asian Traditions**, ask who leads (voice, fiddle, pipes, percussion), whether dance drives repertoire, and how revival institutions shaped what you hear on modern recordings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "instruments-and-voices-in-this-lesson",
      "title": "Instruments and voices in this lesson",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Lead voices: **veena, violin, voice**. Ensemble layer: **mridangam, ensemble**. Compare timbre and role with the anchor unit before assuming techniques transfer unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "forms-dance-and-rhythm",
      "title": "Forms, dance, and rhythm",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Named forms include **raga, tala, kriti**. Dance context: **Bharatanatyam link**. Metre and foot-tap feel are not decorative — they explain why musicians accent certain beats and phrase repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "history-transmission-and-today",
      "title": "History, transmission, and today",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Historical threads: **lineage transmission**. Contemporary scenes: **Cleveland aradhana**. Tune repertoire anchor: **Kriti repertoire**. Follow lesson playlists and reading lists for recorded examples."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "listening-habits",
      "title": "Listening habits",
      "level": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "1. Queue one field or archive recording and one contemporary ensemble track.\n2. Log metre, lead instrument, ornament, and social context in four columns.\n3. Return to the parallel anchor lesson if comparison feels abstract.\n4. Avoid generic world-music compilations when building ear training."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "study-facts",
      "title": "Study facts",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Kriti repertoire\n- Lead instruments: veena, violin, voice\n- Ensemble: mridangam, ensemble\n- Forms: raga, tala, kriti\n- Dance: Bharatanatyam link\n- History: lineage transmission\n- Scenes: Cleveland aradhana\n- Repertoire: Kriti repertoire\n- Compared with Hindustani tradition: regional accent and social context differ.\n- Revival movements and media shaped twentieth-century practice.\n- Dance rhythm and tune metre are linked in this tradition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "key-points",
      "title": "Key points",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "- Carnatic Music is a living tradition with regional accent.\n- Focus: Kriti repertoire.\n- Compared with Hindustani tradition: shared heritage, distinct emphasis.\n- Forms: raga, tala, kriti.\n- Dance: Bharatanatyam link.\n- Transmission: lineage transmission."
        }
      ]
    },
